  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nLight showers continue to stream in from the southwest this\nafternoon amid a passing mid-level disturbance. Observing\nstations within the forecast area average around a quarter of an\ninch in rainfall amounts thus far today, with a few stations in\nsoutheast Ohio closer to half an inch. Forecast trends depict\nshowers gradually petering out through this evening, with skies\nclearing out overnight into Tuesday.\n\nThe Central Appalachians will be on the western flank of high\npressure parked over the Delmarva region, yielding dry weather\nand warming temperatures for Tuesday. Afternoon highs are\nprogged to stretch into the upper 70s across the lowlands and\n60s to low 70s along the higher terrain.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry weather will be fleeting on Tuesday, as the next disturbance\ntakes aim for the area heading into midweek. This will be in the\nform of a nearly stacked low pressure system trekking into the\nGreat Lakes with a cold front stemming down into the Ohio and\nTennessee Valleys. Global models remain in decent agreement of\nshowers and isolated storms encroaching ahead of the front\nbeginning around Wednesday afternoon. Precipitation chances will\nprevail into the overnight hours and Thursday morning as the\nfront passes overhead. \n\nDespite the front's complete passage, low end precipitation\nchances prosper into the end of the work week for at least the\nhigher terrain. Temperatures settle back into their seasonable\nnorms Thursday and Friday before a bigger push of cold weather\narrives for this weekend. A strengthening upper level trough\nbarrels down into the Ohio Valley by the start of the weekend,\nwith plunging temperatures moving down from the north. This will\nreintroduce sub-freezing overnight lows for the bulk of the\nforecast area beginning late Saturday night into next week, with\ndaytime highs hovering in the 50s/60s.",
